  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1111 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe upper level low continues to drift northeastward, with water \nvapor satellite suggesting the center is somewhere near Berthoud\nPass as of 05z. This circulation is also evident on KFTG/KCYS, \nwith bands of rain and snow showers pivoting to the west/southwest\nover Larimer and Jackson counties, while most of the snow showers\nalong the I-70 corridor had moved eastward over the last couple \nof hours. Meanwhile, there's been some patchy dense fog that's \ndeveloped across the lower foothills and portions of the I-25 \ncorridor. Models do not have a very good handle on where and how \nlong this will last, but it seems plausible that this would \ncontinue for the next few hours before quickly diminishing \nsometime in the early AM.\n\nAlmost all of the additional snowfall tonight will fall along and\nnorth of the track of the 700/500mb lows overnight, meaning \nlittle to no additional snowfall is expected along and south of \nI-70. Even our far northern mountains may only see lower end\nadvisory totals through tomorrow morning, with most guidance\nkeeping the more significant QPF in Wyoming. We've tweaked the\ntiming of the Winter Weather Advisories to try to account for\nthis.\n\nRain and snow showers will likely be ongoing across the northern\nCWA Thursday morning, with a band of precipitation pivoting from\nnorthwest to southeast during the morning hours as the upper low\ntreks off to the northeast. Any additional precipitation across\nthe I-25 corridor is expected to be light (generally just a few\nhundredths). Winds will pick up during the morning hours and\ncontinue through the afternoon as 800-700mb flow increases to\n30-40kt in a subsident/cold air advection regime. A few gusts of\n40-45 mph will be possible along and east of I-25 during the late\nmorning and most of the afternoon.\n\nNorth-northwest flow aloft will continue on Friday, but winds are\nexpected to diminish as the mid-level flow weakens and QG\nsubsidence also decreases. We'll be left with a relatively cool\nbut sunny day with highs remaining in the mid 50s to perhaps low \n60s across the plains. \n\nA broad upper ridge will then be the focus for the weekend, with a\npronounced warmup likely for both Saturday and Sunday. Highs\nacross the plains are expected to rebound into the 70s, with dry\nconditions. While winds will generally remain weak, there could be\nsome locally critical fire weather conditions over the far eastern\nplains both Saturday and Sunday afternoon.\n\nBy next week, guidance generally begins to push the ridge off to\nthe east, leaving the region in weak southwesterly flow aloft. A\nweak shortwave or two may attempt to push into the area by\nearly/mid week, leading to a gradual increase in rain and snow\nshowers across the higher elevations. However, the chances of any\nmeaningful precipitation looks rather low... with 90th percentile\nQPF less than a tenth of an inch across the mountains through\nWednesday. Temperatures during this period will likely remain\nabove normal with highs in the 70s most days. There are hints of \na more organized storm system by this time next week, but there \n(unsurprisingly) rather large differences in timing and location \nof that potential system at this time.",
